    "content": "Axe to Fall was tracked by Kurt Ballou at GodCity Studio in Salem, Massachusetts and mixed by Matt Bayles (Mastodon, Isis, Botch, Pearl Jam) at his Red Room Recording in Seattle. The two-stage production gave the album its distinctive character — Ballou's immediate, close-miked tracking captured the band's live energy, while Bayles' mix sat the drums tight against the dense guitar layers and accommodated the wide range of guest contributions.\n\n**Microphone Setup (Tracking at GodCity):**\n- Kick drum: Shure Beta 52 inside, AKG D112 outside\n- Snare: Shure SM57 top, condenser bottom\n- Toms: Sennheiser MD421 on each tom\n- Hi-hat: AKG C451 small-diaphragm condenser\n- Overheads: Two AKG C414 large-diaphragm condensers\n- Room mics: Ribbon mics for natural room ambience\n\n**Studio Environment:**\nGodCity by 2009 was a more developed studio than the Jane Doe-era room — same close-miked philosophy, more refined acoustics, and a wider mic locker. The result is a slightly fuller drum sound than Jane Doe, while preserving the immediacy of Ballou's tracking approach.\n\n**Mix Approach (Bayles at Red Room):**\nMatt Bayles is known for sitting drums tight against the guitars without losing transient detail. His Axe to Fall mix keeps Koller's snare and kick forward in the arrangement, with the toms clearly differentiated for the album's many fill-heavy moments. The guest contributions — vocals, additional guitars, electronics — are layered around the rhythm section without crowding the kit.\n\n**Performance Approach:**\nKoller tracked Axe to Fall in mostly live takes, the same approach used on Jane Doe. The collaborative tracks were built around scratch performances, with guest contributions overdubbed afterward.",
